\title{P9 - The gap to the leaders on navier-stokes-euler, cslib and con-leche, declaration by declaration}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{reference-engine}
\tag{performance}
\tag{benchmark}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/P9.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Profiler P9, 2026-11-24 (21:08Z - 21:55Z). No production code was changed. Scratch, tools and raw data: \code{\verb~|work/prof/P9/~}. Living summary: \code{\verb~|work/status/prof-P9.md~}. Board lines: \code{\verb~|work/status/requests.log~} (21:16Z, 21:26Z, 21:47Z). Model and tools: profile P8 ([rep-P8](rep-P8), \code{\verb~|work/prof/P8/w9/~}).}

\p{Question: the machine's CPU time against gruszka (integrator's table, fresh process) is 3.9 times on navier-stokes-euler, 1.8 times on cslib, 1.3 times on con-leche. P8 explained Mathlib's 3.0 times by the official way of comparing proofs (40 \% of the machine's work) and by the eager-reduction declines (10-12 \%). What is it on navier-stokes-euler: the same two mechanisms in another proportion, or something new? And does a class of declarations stick out on cslib and con-leche?}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus}\th{units}\th{machine, instructions in the checks}\th{gruszka, same units}\th{ratio}\th{nano-lean ratio}\th{(a) removed by \code{\verb~|congruence,skipargs~} (NOT official-exact)}\th{(b) eager-reduction declines: units, share of the worker's CPU}\th{(c) the rest}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{124,850}\td{886.4 G}\td{111.0 G}\td{\strong{7.98}}\td{10.83}\td{\strong{54.5 \%} of the instructions, 51.2 \% of the worker's CPU}\td{251 units, 1.5 \%}\td{47.3 \% of the CPU: 3.63 times gruszka in instructions}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{397,608}\td{587.0 G}\td{142.4 G}\td{\strong{4.12}}\td{5.18}\td{22.4 \% of the instructions, 14.6 \% of the worker's CPU}\td{\strong{2,406 units, 12.6 \%}}\td{3.20 times}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{36,274}\td{146.9 G}\td{45.3 G}\td{\strong{3.24}}\td{2.85}\td{none: +9.2 \% instructions, +5.6 \% CPU of the checks (the options COST there)}\td{88 units, 2.9 \%}\td{3.24 times, flat}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib (P8, the step-1 set of this morning)}\td{714,712}\td{2,438.6 G}\td{373.8 G}\td{6.52}\td{7.64}\td{40.5 \%}\td{6,711 units, 11-12 \%}\td{3.88 times}}
}

\p{(One worker, native -O3, the machine's default switch set of the published jars of 21:02Z, instructions and thread CPU inside \code{\verb~|Session.check~} per unit; gruszka and nano-lean per declaration with the arena builds, measured in the same hour; CPU shares from one \src{bench/quiet.py} window, section 4.8.)}

\ol{
\li{\strong{navier-stokes-euler is the same mechanism as Mathlib in a larger dose; nothing about numerals.} 54.5 \% of the machine's instructions there (51 \% of the worker's CPU time on quiet cores: 29.7 of 58.1 s) disappear with the two non-official options; with them the machine is at 3.63 times gruszka, which is where P8 found Mathlib with them (3.88, on this morning's switch set) and close to where con-leche and cslib are (3.2 - 3.5).}
\li{\strong{Where}: 84 \% of the machine's instructions are in the PROJECT'S OWN 57,330 units, at 9.35 times gruszka (its Mathlib part: 4.63, Init: 2.58). Worst: the 690 \code{\verb~|Euler*~} namespaces (25,646 units, 57.7 \% of the machine's instructions, 13.4 times). Units at 8 times and more are 14.2 \% of the units and 61.6 \% of the machine's instructions (19.8 times as a class); the units below 8 are at 4.08 times (P8 found the same 4.08 on Mathlib, with this morning's switch set).}
\li{\strong{What is compared}: (i) proofs by their types (the official rule), as on Mathlib, but the proofs are those of analysis: the two instance paths of the reals (\code{\verb~|Real.normedField ..~} against \code{\verb~|Real.semiring~} / \code{\verb~|Real.commRing~}), bundled maps (\code{\verb~|LinearMap.instAdd._proof_1/2~}, \code{\verb~|ContinuousLinearMap.add._proof_1~}, \code{\verb~|MultilinearMap~}, \code{\verb~|BoundedContinuousFunction~}) and subobjects (\code{\verb~|AddMemClass.add._proof_1~}: elements of \code{\verb~|Lp~}); (ii) a second class that \code{\verb~|skipargs~} does not touch and only \code{\verb~|congruence~} repairs, 14 \% of the corpus: applications of ONE HEAD or ONE TERM UNDER TWO ENVIRONMENTS, unfolded on both sides instead of compared by their arguments. The extreme unit (\code{\verb~|EulerSobolevBaseCommutator.baseCommutator_bound~}, 1.54 M ticks against gruszka's 13 k) compares a sum over a list of 5,461 Sobolev words with itself under another environment, element by element (section 4.2).}
\li{\strong{What it is not}: Nat / Int literal arithmetic is 70,600 folding events and recursor reduction 277,300 IOTA events of 553 M (nano-lean counts 277,600 iota steps and 57,500 literal operations, 12 of them on big numbers); units in which recursors and literals are 3 \% or more of the events carry 0.3 \% of the machine's instructions; structure eta is 12,400 ETAS events. The large \code{\verb~|Finset.sum~} / \code{\verb~|Matrix.vecCons~} proofs (\code{\verb~|quadraticBilin._proof_N~}, the top 4 units for everybody) are at 3.7 times with the same number of operations as gruszka (section 4.6): cost per operation, not a missing rule. A sum is the cost in one unit only, and there it is the conversion of point 3 (ii).}
\li{\strong{Eager-reduction declines are small on navier-stokes-euler (251 units, 0.87 s of 58 s) and as large as on Mathlib on cslib: 2,406 units, 5.9 s of 46.6 s (12.6 \% of the worker)}, for units that cost gruszka 1.0 s.}
\li{\strong{cslib is Mathlib at a smaller dose, nothing sticks out}: 79 \% of the cost is in the 297,351 units of Mathlib and its dependencies (4.39 times; the options remove 27.5 \% there), the project's own 14,249 units are 3 \% of the cost at 2.63 times. \strong{con-leche is flat}: every kind of unit with more than 1 \% of the cost is between 2.6 and 3.9 times, the machine makes 1.30 times gruszka's ticks and 0.62 times its values; the factor is instructions per operation (1,979 per tick against 793). One unit is 5.2 \% of the corpus (\code{\verb~|ConLeche.Cached.reduceNatI._mutual.eq_def~}, 7.9 \% for gruszka).}
\li{\strong{The two options are not a free win}: on con-leche \code{\verb~|congruence~} costs 8.4 \% of the instructions (that one unit goes from 7.65 G to 20.67 G: the congruence walks cost more than the unfoldings they replace, section 4.7). Whoever weighs an opt-in profile needs that row.}
\li{\strong{In CPU time the ratios are smaller than in instructions} because the machine retires 15 to 18 G instructions per second inside the checks and gruszka 6 to 7: about 3.2 (navier-stokes-euler), 1.6 (cslib), 1.2 (con-leche) for the checks alone (gruszka's side is its own clock from the profile run, not a quiet measurement).}
}

\p{Numbers are instruction and event counts unless a unit of time is given. CPU seconds are thread CPU clocks of one \src{bench/quiet.py} window (section 4.8) unless marked otherwise.}
}

