\subtree{
\title{4. Mechanisms}

\subtree{
\title{4.1 Proofs are compared by their types (the official rule); on navier-stokes-euler they are the proofs of analysis}

\p{\strong{Size: the 53.4 \% + 5.7 \% rows above; \code{\verb~|skipargs~} alone removes 42.2 \% of the machine's navier-stokes-euler instructions (57.8 \% of its ticks), 17.7 \% on cslib, 1.8 \% on con-leche.} Owner of the place: M4 (\code{\verb~|Conv.java~}, \code{\verb~|Cargs.java~}), facts by M5 (\code{\verb~|Facts.java~}); the rule that keeps it off: NOTES-M4 D27, findings S-F1 / S-F2, owner note 11; reviewed as theory-sound and not official in [rep-soundness-shortcuts](rep-soundness-shortcuts); agent C1 found no official-exact route to it ([rep-exact-proof-conversion](rep-exact-proof-conversion)).}

\p{Traced unit (\code{\verb~|out/trace-nse-def.txt~}): \code{\verb~|EulerBoundedFieldGramInverse.inversePath._proof_1~}, a project theorem at 161 times.}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{ticks / steps}\th{instructions}\th{unfoldings}\th{conversion problems}\th{inference}}
\tr{\td{machine, default set}\td{1,199,526}\td{1.69 G}\td{142,742 DELTA + 43,802 memo hits}\td{34,564 CONV + 8,506 pair hits, 32,363 PIRR, 41,798 VTYPE}\td{524 INFER, 48 CHECK}}
\tr{\td{machine + \code{\verb~|facts~} + \code{\verb~|skipargs~}}\td{16,517}\td{40.4 M}\td{}\td{}\td{}}
\tr{\td{machine + \code{\verb~|facts,probes~} + \code{\verb~|congruence~}}\td{28,255}\td{63.6 M}\td{}\td{}\td{}}
\tr{\td{machine + both}\td{12,667}\td{33.7 M}\td{769 DELTA + 319}\td{881 CONV + 1,060, 31 PIRR, 502 VTYPE}\td{514 INFER, 48 CHECK}}
\tr{\td{gruszka}\td{9,867}\td{10.5 M}\td{716}\td{1,319 def_eq, 673 cache hits, 89 closure congruences}\td{974 infer misses}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean}\td{20,462}\td{10.0 M}\td{762}\td{5,512 def_eq, 4,353 pointer-equal}\td{894 infer misses}}
}

\p{What happens, from the trace:}

\ul{
\li{Events 1 - 27: the theorem's type is inferred; \code{\verb~|NormedSpace Real E (DenselyNormedField.toNormedField (RCLike.to.. Real.instRCLike)) ..~} is compared with \code{\verb~|NormedSpace Real E Real.normedField ..~}, then \code{\verb~|Module Real E (DivisionSemiring.toSemiring (.. NormedField.toField Real.normedField)) ..~} with \code{\verb~|Module Real E Real.semiring ..~}. Two instance paths to the semiring of \code{\verb~|Real~}: lazy delta unfolds both (\code{\verb~|Real.normedField~}, \code{\verb~|Real.semiring~}, \code{\verb~|inferInstance~}, \code{\verb~|Real.instCommSemiring~}, \code{\verb~|Real.commRing~} ...).}
\li{Events 28 - 90: the two unfolded structures are compared field by field: \code{\verb~|AddCommMonoid.mk Real (..) Real.normedField._proof_1 =?= AddCommMonoid.mk Real (AddMonoid.mk ..) (fun a b => Real.commRing._proof_6 a b)~}. The proof field: CONV, not identical, \strong{PIRR}: both types are inferred and compared: \code{\verb~|(a b : Real) -> a + b = b + a~} with \code{\verb~|+~} through \code{\verb~|SubNegMonoid.toAddMonoid (.. NormedAddCommGroup.toAddCommGroup Real.normedAddCommGroup)~} against the same with \code{\verb~|Real.instAdd~}. \code{\verb~|HAdd.hAdd~}, \code{\verb~|instHAdd~}, \code{\verb~|Add.add~}, \code{\verb~|AddSemigroup.toAdd~} are projection functions (hint abbrev): the official order (\code{\verb~|refs/lean4/src/kernel/type_checker.cpp:995~}, arguments first only for REGULAR definitions) unfolds both sides down to the structure, for every \code{\verb~|+~}, \code{\verb~|*~}, \code{\verb~|0~} of every law (\code{\verb~|mul_zero~}, \code{\verb~|zero_mul~} follow).}
\li{The rest of the unit is the same pattern on bundled maps: a sum of continuous linear maps unfolds to \code{\verb~|ContinuousLinearMap.mk (LinearMap.mk (AddHom.mk (fun x => f x + g x) proof) proof) proof~}, and two such values are compared constructor by constructor: \code{\verb~|LinearMap.instAdd._proof_1 =?= LinearMap.instAdd._proof_1~} (572 times), \code{\verb~|LinearMap.instAdd._proof_2~} (572), \code{\verb~|ContinuousLinearMap.add._proof_1~} (572; the unit poses 967 \code{\verb~|Continuous =?= Continuous~} problems), \code{\verb~|BoundedContinuousFunction.instAdd._proof_1/2~} (132 each), \code{\verb~|LinearMap.instSMul._proof_*~}, \code{\verb~|ContinuousLinearMap.neg._proof_1~} ... Each type mentions \code{\verb~|DFunLike.coe~} and \code{\verb~|+~} on both sides: of the 142,742 DELTA events 25,467 are \code{\verb~|HAdd.hAdd~}, 25,458 \code{\verb~|Add.add~}, 24,420 \code{\verb~|DFunLike.coe~}, 17,447 \code{\verb~|AddHom.toFun~}, 10,374 \code{\verb~|SMul.smul~}, 9,834 \code{\verb~|HSMul.hSMul~}, 7,168 \code{\verb~|Neg.neg~}, 4,147 \code{\verb~|LinearMap.toAddHom~}, 4,094 \code{\verb~|ContinuousLinearMap.toLinearMap~}; of the 34,564 conversion problems 10,437 are \code{\verb~|DFunLike.coe =?= DFunLike.coe~}, 6,653 \code{\verb~|HAdd.hAdd~}, 1,705 \code{\verb~|Eq~}, 1,704 Pi. Of the events in the 30 most frequent judgment chains, 72 \% of the CONV events and 40 \% of the DELTA events have a PIRR among the 12 innermost judgments on the host stack (a lower bound: the stack is cut at 12 labels and at frames).}
}

\p{Other traced units of this class (\code{\verb~|scripts/tracesum.py~}): \code{\verb~|AnalyticOnNhd.iteratedFDeriv~} (Mathlib; 496,077 ticks against gruszka's 25,061; proofs compared: \code{\verb~|ContinuousMultilinearMap.instAdd._proof_1~} 410, \code{\verb~|MultilinearMap.instAdd._proof_1/2~} 408 each, \code{\verb~|AddCommGroup.add_comm~} 194; 79 \% of the CONV events below a PIRR), \code{\verb~|EulerLpCylinderCoefficients.liftedOperatorPathMap~} (a project definition at 570 times: 413,359 ticks against 1,049; \code{\verb~|AddMemClass.add._proof_1~} 676 times - the membership proof of a sum of two elements of \code{\verb~|Lp~}, a subtype of \code{\verb~|AEEqFun~} -, \code{\verb~|MeasureTheory.Lp.instModule._proof_7~}, \code{\verb~|AddSubgroupClass.instZSMul._proof_1~}; DELTA \code{\verb~|Subtype.val~} 5,258, \code{\verb~|Membership.mem~} 4,466; with both options 751 ticks). So the three families of proofs on navier-stokes-euler are: the instance paths of \code{\verb~|Real~} and of the normed structures, the proof fields of bundled maps, the membership proofs of subobjects.}

\p{What the leaders do instead (lines verified in \code{\verb~|refs/~}): gruszka \code{\verb~|src/tc.rs~}: \code{\verb~|spines_defeq_skip~} (8573: skips \code{\verb~|prop_positions~}, 8641, and inside lazy delta \code{\verb~|unused_args~}, 8603), \code{\verb~|try_eta_struct~} (8823) with the same mask, \code{\verb~|surely_not_proof~} (7297), \code{\verb~|proof_irrel~} (7499) only for what is left; nano-lean \code{\verb~|src/value_checker/conv.rs~}: \code{\verb~|relevant~} (389), \code{\verb~|probe_args~} (359), \code{\verb~|statically_not_proof~} (315). Official-exact? \strong{No} (S-F2: the official \code{\verb~|is_def_eq~} is not a congruence where a reduction inspects a proof; \code{\verb~|M4OfficialTest~}). The machine has the same masks behind \code{\verb~|skipargs~} (B3, B4, the Prop mask of ETAS; statistics of the options run on navier-stokes-euler: B3 answered 494,369 times, B4 2,406,940 times, B1 4,124,373, B12 916,448).}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.2 Congruence before unfolding: the same head, the same term under two environments (only \code{\verb~|congruence~} repairs it)}

\p{\strong{Size on navier-stokes-euler: 6,277 units, 14.0 \% of the machine's instructions, 11.8 times gruszka; 12.3 \% of the corpus is removed only when \code{\verb~|congruence~} is on} (cslib 4.6 \%; on Mathlib P8's figures give 9 \%). Owner: M4 (\code{\verb~|Conv.congruence~}: B1 same-head probe, B12 closure congruence, B2; \code{\verb~|Same.java~}). Off since D27 (S-F1).}

\p{Traced unit (\code{\verb~|out/trace-nse-def-long.txt~}, \code{\verb~|trace-nse-def-mid.txt~}): \code{\verb~|EulerSobolevBaseCommutator.baseCommutator_bound~}, the unit with the largest excess of the corpus.}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{ticks / steps}\th{instructions}\th{unfoldings}\th{recursor steps}\th{conversion problems}}
\tr{\td{machine, default set}\td{1,537,192}\td{2.17 G}\td{89,368 DELTA}\td{53,288 IOTA, 353 QUOTR}\td{30,439 CONV, 29,743 PIRR}}
\tr{\td{machine + \code{\verb~|facts~} + \code{\verb~|skipargs~}}\td{1,450,455}\td{2.11 G}\td{}\td{}\td{}}
\tr{\td{machine + \code{\verb~|facts,probes~} (official-exact)}\td{2,025,399}\td{2.60 G}\td{}\td{}\td{}}
\tr{\td{machine + \code{\verb~|facts,probes~} + \code{\verb~|congruence~}}\td{41,200}\td{84.4 M}\td{}\td{}\td{}}
\tr{\td{machine + both}\td{27,988}\td{65.6 M}\td{2,546 DELTA}\td{313 IOTA}\td{1,791 CONV, 9 PIRR}}
\tr{\td{gruszka}\td{12,998}\td{47.9 M}\td{1,663}\td{}\td{917 def_eq, 476 cache hits, \strong{110 closure congruences}}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean}\td{34,155}\td{16.3 M}\td{2,600 (+ 904 derived rules)}\td{352 iota}\td{7,106 def_eq, 5,110 pointer-equal, 220 probes}}
}

\p{What happens:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The bulk (events 60,000 and after; a cycle of nine events): two sums over one list are compared element by element.} Both sides are \code{\verb~|List.foldr (fun a acc => a + acc) 0 l~} where \code{\verb~|l~} is a list of norms indexed by \code{\verb~|EulerCylinderSobolevSpace.SobolevWord~}s (a Sobolev norm written as a sum over words) and the summand closure differs only in its environment. Per element: \code{\verb~|List.foldr._f~} is unfolded on both sides, its matcher on both sides, \code{\verb~|HAdd.hAdd~} and \code{\verb~|Add.add~} on both sides, the two heads are compared (\code{\verb~|MeasureTheory.eLpNorm (AEEqFun.cast f) .. =?= MeasureTheory.eLpNorm (AEEqFun.cast g) ..~}: a regular definition, arguments first, down to \code{\verb~|Subtype.val~}), then the two tails. The counts give the length: \code{\verb~|List.foldr._f~} 10,922 unfoldings = 2 x 5,461, \code{\verb~|eLpNorm =?= eLpNorm~} 5,464, \code{\verb~|Norm.norm~} 10,948, \code{\verb~|Subtype.val~} 22,245, \code{\verb~|HAdd.hAdd~} 11,741, \code{\verb~|Add.add~} 11,739, 53,288 recursor steps (9.8 per element). 5,461 = (4^7 - 1) / 3, the number of words of length at most 6 over four letters. So: one comparison of a 5,461-term sum with itself under another environment is 1.5 M ticks. This is the only place where a \code{\verb~|Finset~} / list sum is the cost on this corpus, and it is conversion, not evaluation.}
\li{\strong{The prelude (events 3,636 - 3,672) shows the closure case in small}: the metric of \code{\verb~|EuclideanSpace Real (Fin 3)~} (\code{\verb~|PiLp 2 fun _ : Fin 3 => Real~}) through two instance paths; the comparison reaches the definition of the distance, \code{\verb~|ite (2 = top) (iSup ..) ((Finset.sum univ fun i => dist (x i) (y i) ^ p.toReal) ^ (1 / p.toReal))~}, the same lambda on both sides under two environments whose entries are convertible but not identical. \code{\verb~|HPow.hPow~} is a projection function: both sides are unfolded (\code{\verb~|instHPow~}, \code{\verb~|Pow.pow~}, \code{\verb~|Real.instPow~}), then \code{\verb~|Finset.sum =?= Finset.sum~} (regular: arguments first), the two lambdas are opened (CBIND), \code{\verb~|HPow.hPow (dist ..)~} is unfolded again, \code{\verb~|Dist.dist~} down to \code{\verb~|PseudoMetricSpace.toDist Real (..)~}, then the other branch \code{\verb~|iSup ..~}, \code{\verb~|Finset.card (Set.Finite.toFinset ..)~}.}
\li{\code{\verb~|skipargs~} leaves the unit as it is (1,450,455 ticks) and only 2 \% of the events of the 30 most frequent chains are below a PIRR: it is not a proof-position case.}
}

\p{Second traced unit, more typical of the class: \code{\verb~|NavierStokes.AxisContraction.natural_axis_profiles~} (894,995 ticks, 1.17 G; \code{\verb~|skipargs~} 622 M, \code{\verb~|congruence~} 127 M, both 100 M; gruszka 26.3 M). 40,569 of its 93,580 DELTA events are in the chain \code{\verb~|DELTA < LSTEP < LAZYD < CONV < CARGS < LSTEP < LAZYD < CONV < CARGS < LSTEP < LAZYD~}: applications of one head whose arguments are applications of one head ..., with projection functions unfolded at every level (\code{\verb~|DFunLike.coe~} 14,540, \code{\verb~|HAdd.hAdd~} 10,240, \code{\verb~|Add.add~} 10,237, \code{\verb~|ContinuousMap.toFun~} 6,055, \code{\verb~|BoundedContinuousFunction.toContinuousMap~} 3,146); 30 \% of the CONV events are below a PIRR (\code{\verb~|BoundedContinuousFunction.instAdd._proof_1/2~}, 634 each).}

\p{What the leaders do instead: gruszka \code{\verb~|closure_congr~} (\code{\verb~|tc.rs:6792~}, at the entry of every comparison, 6650 / 6713) and \code{\verb~|same_term~} (7026): two closures or thunks of the same term and level substitution are equal when the environment entries the term uses are identical or already known equal; \code{\verb~|quick_parts~} (8543) after \code{\verb~|whnf_core~} and inside the lazy delta loops (7251, 8102, 8517). nano-lean: \code{\verb~|same(t, s, fuel)~} (\code{\verb~|conv.rs:107~}) and the same-head probe \code{\verb~|probe_args~} (359) before \code{\verb~|whnf_core~}, for every unfoldable head. The official kernel tries the arguments of two applications of one head first only for REGULAR definitions (\code{\verb~|type_checker.cpp:995-1002~}), never for a projection function, and has no closure comparison. Official-exact? \strong{No} (S-F1). The machine's default set contains the official-exact part (\code{\verb~|same~}, D28: the walk answers only for identical slots; B12 answered T 957,386 times and U 9,511,740 times in the default run): the gap is "entries convertible, not identical" and "same head, abbreviation".}

\p{Other units of the class: \code{\verb~|EulerDriftGlobalInviscidGevrey.exists_global_inviscid_gevrey_PDE~} (792 M -> 216 M with \code{\verb~|congruence~}; \code{\verb~|skipargs~} 632 M), \code{\verb~|EulerCylinderDirichlet.Coefficients.coordinate_equation_ae~} (828 M -> 326 M; 532 M), \code{\verb~|EulerAllOrderDriftCorrection.finite_exists~} (763 M -> 179 M; 648 M).}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.3 Eager-reduction declines (M6-F26)}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus}\th{declined units}\th{of which}\th{machine before the decline (both attempts)}\th{reference engine after them (quiet)}\th{share of the worker}\th{reference steps}\th{the same units for gruszka}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{251 (0.2 \%)}\td{249 \code{\verb~|_proof_N~}; 247 Mathlib, 4 Init}\td{0.04 s, 482 M instr}\td{0.87 s}\td{1.5 \%}\td{10.3 M}\td{891 M instr = 0.8 \% of its corpus, 0.13 s}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{2,406 (0.6 \%)}\td{2,273 \code{\verb~|_proof_N~}, 118 other theorems; 1,614 Mathlib, 594 the project's, 164 Init, 34 Std}\td{0.27 s, 3.77 G}\td{\strong{5.89 s}}\td{\strong{12.6 \%}}\td{18.0 M}\td{6.55 G = 4.6 \%, 1.04 s}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{88 (0.2 \%)}\td{83 \code{\verb~|_proof_N~}; 81 the project's, 7 Init}\td{0.07 s, 1.27 G}\td{0.25 s}\td{2.9 \%}\td{0.86 M}\td{451 M = 1.0 \%, 0.07 s}}
}

\p{Every declined unit is declined twice (warm attempt, cold retry; the second costs the machine nothing: 13 us between the two) and then decided by the reference engine: per unit a median of 1.5 ms on cslib (10 \% of them under 0.58 ms, 10 \% above 5.2 ms, the largest about 70 ms: \code{\verb~|..ContinuousLinearMap.norm_eq_iSup_rayleighQuotient._proof_1_5~} and \code{\verb~|..Cslib.Automata.NA.loop_fin_run_exists._proof_1_88~}). The cost is not proportional to the reference steps (7.5 k steps per unit on cslib, 41 k on navier-stokes-euler, 2.4 ms and 3.5 ms per unit). Owner: M6 (\code{\verb~|MachineSession~}, \code{\verb~|Machine.enterER~}); E1's prototype ([rep-eager-reduction](rep-eager-reduction), switch \code{\verb~|eager~}) keeps these units on the machine and reports a third of the reference engine's time for them on Std; on cslib that would be about 4 s of 46.6 s. Official-exactness is the reason for the rule.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.4 Between the checks: inductive blocks}

\p{The CPU time between two checks that is neither a decline nor a session reset (quiet window): 2.58 s on navier-stokes-euler (4.4 \% of the worker), 2.04 s on cslib (4.4 \%), 0.53 s on con-leche (6.0 \%). The median gap is 1 to 2 us; the time is in the gaps in which the driver admits declarations that no engine checks (inductive blocks, \code{\verb~|rlvm-inductive~}): navier-stokes-euler 1,355 such gaps, \strong{1.98 s (1.46 ms each)}; cslib 3,218 gaps, 0.58 s; con-leche 301 gaps, 0.27 s. gruszka spends 0.27 s / 0.21 s / 0.08 s (its own clock) on ALL its declarations that are not machine units (1,618 / 4,377 / 473; 1.70 G / 1.22 G / 0.62 G instructions). The project's structures of navier-stokes-euler (\code{\verb~|EulerAllOrderDriftCorrection.ExactLiftedPacket~}: 35.6 M instructions for gruszka, 113 M for nano-lean) are the large ones. Session resets cost 0.33 s / 0.05 s / 0.03 s (7,105 / 6,167 / 1,268 resets seen between checks). Owner: \code{\verb~|rlvm-inductive~} / driver, not the machine. Size: 3.4 \% of the worker on navier-stokes-euler.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.5 Sessions: sibling proofs (con-leche)}

\p{con-leche's units far above the corpus ratio (16 times and more: 1,064 units, 3.2 \% of the machine's instructions) are 427 \code{\verb~|_proof_N_N~}, 104 \code{\verb~|eq_N~}, 342 \code{\verb~|_sparseCasesOn_N~}, 106 \code{\verb~|match_N~} units: families of declarations with shared sub-terms. \code{\verb~|..ConLeche.Model.ctorsLoopAt._proof_1_9 / _10 / _11~}: gruszka 4,538 / 427 / 413 ticks (6.19 M / 0.32 M / 0.35 M instructions), the machine 47,976 / 44,847 / 44,592 ticks (82 - 88 M each): gruszka's later siblings are answered from what the first one left in the session (\code{\verb~|infer_u~} hits 55, \code{\verb~|udm~} hits 98 of 109 probes), the machine's session was reset (cap 1 MiB). Ticks of con-leche by session cap: 1 MiB (default) 74.2 M, 4 MiB 67.4 M (-9.2 \%), 16 MiB 62.0 M (-16.4 \%), 64 MiB 58.6 M (-21.1 \%); \code{\verb~|_proof_1_10~} 44,847 -> 10,170 -> 9,079 ticks, \code{\verb~|_proof_1_11~} 44,592 -> 498 -> 435. The integrator chose 1 MiB on the warm wall clock of Mathlib with 8 workers, and the quiet window confirms it for con-leche with one worker: thread CPU of the checks 7.86 s at 1 MiB, 8.02 s at 4 MiB, 8.61 s at 16 MiB, 10.16 s at 64 MiB (139 and 174 ns per tick against 106): the ticks fall and the time rises. As the sessions are built today these units are not a prize in time. On navier-stokes-euler the same kind of unit is what remains above 8 times WITH the options (6.8 \% of that run's instructions; \code{\verb~|EulerTransversePacketPrimary.Budget.future_derivative_bound~}: 17,589 ticks against gruszka's 744, 9 unfoldings, 171 \code{\verb~|infer_u~} hits).}
}

