\p{The comparison judgments themselves are small; what the proof comparisons cost is evaluation, hash-consing and the stepper for the terms they unfold. \code{\verb~|Same~} is 6 to 8 \% of the instructions wherever it is on. This part is the subject of the function-level profiles (P1, P5, P6), not of this report.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.7 The options cost on con-leche}

\p{con-leche with \code{\verb~|congruence~}: +8.4 \% instructions (159.2 G against 146.9 G), with both +9.2 \%. One unit carries it: \code{\verb~|ConLeche.Cached.reduceNatI._mutual.eq_def~} (the equation lemma of a mutual structural recursion; 3,772,686 ticks, 64,163 IOTA, 74,782 DELTA; 7.65 G instructions for the machine, 3.56 G for gruszka, 5.21 G for nano-lean; 0.747 s of thread CPU on quiet cores against 0.72 s on gruszka's own clock): 7.65 G -> 20.67 G with \code{\verb~|congruence~} (7,404,544 ticks), unchanged with \code{\verb~|skipargs~}. In that unit the option does what it is for - DELTA 74,782 -> 7,544 events, IOTA 64,163 -> 12,514, PIRR 53,464 -> 2,294 - and pays more for it than it saves: CONV 130,878 -> 1,045,912 events, CBIND 64,655 -> 524,804, CARGS 58,706 -> 512,941, APPLY 398,696 -> 1,849,912, EVAL 606,302 -> 1,455,384 (the probes answer T: over the corpus B1 588,534 T / 13,038 F / 6,489 U, B12 441,008 T / 8,000 F). The closures compared are large case trees of a computational definition; walking two of them slot by slot is more work than reducing them. gruszka on the same unit: 2,476,198 ticks, 35,816 unfoldings, 245,178 def_eq calls, 6,147 closure congruences. \code{\verb~|ConLeche.Frontend.keyAt_eq~} 898 M -> 1.70 G, the \code{\verb~|keyOf_N~} family 350 - 430 M -> 580 - 750 M each. On navier-stokes-euler the units made more expensive sum to 0.6 \% of the corpus.}
}

